/-!
# Rings of finite global dimension: cyclic modules suffice

Let `R` be a ring and let `n : ℕ`. The global dimension of `R` is a supremum over *all*
`R`-modules, but it is already computed by the cyclic modules: this file proves Tag 065T, the
equivalence of "the global dimension of `R` is `< n`", "every `R`-module has projective dimension
`< n`", "every finite `R`-module has projective dimension `< n`" and "every cyclic module `R ⧸ I`
has projective dimension `< n`".

Three of the four implications are formal. The one with content is that the cyclic modules
bound everything, and it is Auslander's argument: well-order the underlying set of a module `M`
and filter `M` by the submodules `M_x` spanned by the initial segments `{y | y ≤ x}`. This
filtration is monotone, it exhausts `M` because `x ∈ M_x`, and its graded piece at `x` is
generated by the single element `x`, because `M_x` is the sum of `⨆_{y < x} M_y` and `R ∙ x`. A
module generated by one element is a quotient `R ⧸ I` of `R`, so Tag 0D1U applies.

## Main results

* `Stacks.GlobalDimension.CyclicModules.solution`: Stacks 065T, the four-way equivalence.

## Implementation notes

The filtration used is the one indexed by the whole of `M`: the set of generators of the source's
proof is taken to be all of `M`, which costs nothing and avoids carrying a subtype. The
well-ordering comes from `exists_wellFoundedLT`, the well-ordering theorem, and the order it puts
on the carrier of `M` bears no relation to the module structure; that is why splitting
`span R (Set.Iic x)` off the earlier segments asks for nothing but a `PartialOrder`.

Only the graded pieces of that filtration are shown to be cyclic; the source's "either zero or
generated by one element" is not a case distinction here, since the zero module is generated by
the single element `0`, and `span R {x} = ⊤` is what the argument produces in both cases.

Item (1) is compared with item (2) through the identity, in `WithBot ℕ∞`, of "the supremum of the
projective dimensions is `< n`" with "each projective dimension is `< n`", valid because `n` is a
natural number; that is the whole difference between the global dimension and a uniform bound.

The universe transfer between the modules of items (2) and (3), which live in an arbitrary universe
`v` with `Small.{v} R`, and the cyclic modules `R ⧸ I` of item (4), which live in `R`'s own
universe `u`, is carried by `ModuleCat.projectiveDimension_eq_of_linearEquiv` together with
`CategoryTheory.projectiveDimension_lt_iff`: Mathlib has the transfer of
`HasProjectiveDimensionLE` along a cross-universe linear equivalence but not of
`HasProjectiveDimensionLT`, which is what an `n` allowed to be `0` needs. Item (3) is applied to
`Shrink.{v} (R ⧸ I)`, which is finite over `R` and small because `R ⧸ I` is a quotient of `R`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 065T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/065T)
* M. Auslander, *On the dimension of modules and algebras. III. Global dimension*,
  Nagoya Math. J. **9** (1955), 67-77 — where the argument behind Tag 0D1U is.
-/

namespace Stacks.GlobalDimension.CyclicModules

open _root_.CategoryTheory


/-- In `WithBot ℕ∞`, a supremum is less than a natural number `n` exactly when each of its terms
is: being `< n` there means being `≤ n - 1`, and that passes through a supremum. -/
private lemma iSup_lt_natCast_iff {ι : Sort*} (f : ι → WithBot ℕ∞) (n : ℕ) :
    (⨆ i, f i) < n ↔ ∀ i, f i < n := by
  obtain _ | m := n
  · have h : ∀ x : WithBot ℕ∞, x < ((0 : ℕ) : WithBot ℕ∞) ↔ x ≤ ⊥ :=
      fun x ↦ by simp [le_bot_iff, ← WithBot.lt_coe_bot]
    simp only [h, iSup_le_iff]
  · simp [Nat.cast_succ, ENat.WithBot.lt_add_one_iff, iSup_le_iff]

/-- The global dimension of an abelian category, the supremum of the projective dimensions of its
objects, is less than a natural number `n` exactly when every object has projective dimension
`< n`. -/
private lemma iSup_projectiveDimension_lt_iff {C : Type*} [Category C] [Abelian C] (n : ℕ) :
    (⨆ X : C, projectiveDimension X) < n ↔ ∀ X : C, HasProjectiveDimensionLT X n := by
  simp [iSup_lt_natCast_iff, projectiveDimension_lt_iff]

variable {R : Type u} [Ring R]

/-- `HasProjectiveDimensionLT` transfers along a linear equivalence between modules taken in
different universes. Mathlib has this for `HasProjectiveDimensionLE`, hence for
`HasProjectiveDimensionLT _ (n + 1)`, but not for `n = 0`. -/
private lemma hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_linearEquiv [Small.{v} R] [Small.{w} R]
    {M : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] {N : Type w} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N]
    (e : M ≃ₗ[R] N) (n : ℕ) (h : HasProjectiveDimensionLT (ModuleCat.of R M) n) :
    HasProjectiveDimensionLT (ModuleCat.of R N) n := by
  rw [← projectiveDimension_lt_iff] at h ⊢
  rwa [← ModuleCat.projectiveDimension_eq_of_linearEquiv.{v, w}
    (M := ModuleCat.of R M) (N := ModuleCat.of R N) e]

/-- A module generated by a single element `g` has projective dimension `< n` as soon as every
cyclic module `R ⧸ I` does: it *is* one of them, the quotient of `R` by the annihilator of `g`. -/
private lemma hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_span_singleton_eq_top [Small.{v} R] {Q : Type v}
    [AddCommGroup Q] [Module R Q] (n : ℕ)
    (h : ∀ I : Ideal R, HasProjectiveDimensionLT (ModuleCat.of R (R ⧸ I)) n)
    {g : Q} (hg : Submodule.span R {g} = ⊤) :
    HasProjectiveDimensionLT (ModuleCat.of R Q) n :=
  hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_linearEquiv (LinearMap.quotKerEquivOfSurjective _
    (LinearMap.range_eq_top.mp (by rw [LinearMap.range_toSpanSingleton]; exact hg))) n (h _)

/-- If a submodule `P` of `M` and an element `x` generate `M` between them, then the quotient
`M ⧸ P` is generated by the image of `x`. -/
private lemma span_singleton_mkQ_eq_top {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    {P : Submodule R M} {x : M} (h : P ⊔ Submodule.span R {x} = ⊤) :
    Submodule.span R {P.mkQ x} = ⊤ := by
  simpa [Submodule.map_sup, Submodule.map_span, Submodule.mkQ_map_self]
    using congrArg (Submodule.map P.mkQ) h

/-- If a submodule `N` is the sum of a submodule `P` and the span of an element `x` of `N`, then
the quotient `↥N ⧸ P` is generated by the image of `x`. -/
private lemma span_singleton_mkQ_submoduleOf_eq_top {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    {N P : Submodule R M} {x : M} (hx : x ∈ N) (hN : N = P ⊔ Submodule.span R {x}) :
    Submodule.span R {(P.submoduleOf N).mkQ ⟨x, hx⟩} = ⊤ :=
  span_singleton_mkQ_eq_top <| by
    rw [← LinearMap.submoduleOf_span_singleton_of_mem N hx,
      ← Submodule.submoduleOf_sup_of_le (le_sup_left.trans hN.ge) (le_sup_right.trans hN.ge),
      ← hN, Submodule.submoduleOf_self]

/-- For any partial order on a module, the span of an initial segment `Set.Iic x` is the span of
the strictly earlier segments together with `x`. This is what makes the graded pieces of the
filtration by initial segments cyclic. -/
private lemma span_Iic_eq_biSup_sup_span_singleton {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M]
    [PartialOrder M] (x : M) : Submodule.span R (Set.Iic x)
      = (⨆ y < x, Submodule.span R (Set.Iic y)) ⊔ Submodule.span R {x} := by
  have hIio : (⨆ y < x, Submodule.span R (Set.Iic y)) = Submodule.span R (Set.Iio x) := by
    rw [← Submodule.span_iUnion₂ (s := fun (y : M) (_ : y < x) ↦ Set.Iic y)]
    exact congrArg _ (Set.Subset.antisymm (Set.iUnion₂_subset fun _ hy ↦ Set.Iic_subset_Iio.2 hy)
      fun z hz ↦ Set.mem_biUnion hz Set.self_mem_Iic)
  rw [hIio, sup_comm, ← Submodule.span_union, Set.singleton_union, Set.Iio_insert]

/-- **Auslander's argument.** If every cyclic module `R ⧸ I` has projective dimension `< n`, then
so does every `R`-module: well-order `M`, filter it by the spans `M_x` of the initial segments
`{y | y ≤ x}`, and note that the graded piece of that filtration at `x` is generated by `x`. -/
private theorem hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_forall_ideal [Small.{v} R] (n : ℕ)
    (h : ∀ I : Ideal R, HasProjectiveDimensionLT (ModuleCat.of R (R ⧸ I)) n)
    (M : ModuleCat.{v} R) : HasProjectiveDimensionLT M n := by
  obtain ⟨_, _⟩ := exists_wellFoundedLT ↥M
  have hmem : ∀ x : ↥M, x ∈ Submodule.span R (Set.Iic x) := fun _ ↦
    Submodule.subset_span Set.self_mem_Iic
  exact ModuleCat.hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_monotone_of_iSup_eq_top
    (fun x : ↥M ↦ Submodule.span R (Set.Iic x))
    (fun _ _ hab ↦ Submodule.span_mono (Set.Iic_subset_Iic.2 hab))
    (eq_top_iff.mpr fun m _ ↦ Submodule.mem_iSup_of_mem m (hmem m)) n fun x ↦
    hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_span_singleton_eq_top n h
      (span_singleton_mkQ_submoduleOf_eq_top (hmem x) (span_Iic_eq_biSup_sup_span_singleton x))

/-- For a ring `R` and `n : ℕ`, the following are equivalent: the global dimension of `R` is
`< n`; every `R`-module has projective dimension `< n`; every finite `R`-module does; every
cyclic module `R ⧸ I` does. -/
public theorem solution (R : Type u) [Ring R] [Small.{v} R] (n : ℕ) :
    List.TFAE
      [(⨆ M : ModuleCat.{u} R, projectiveDimension M) < n,
       ∀ M : ModuleCat.{v} R, HasProjectiveDimensionLT M n,
       ∀ M : ModuleCat.{v} R, Module.Finite R M → HasProjectiveDimensionLT M n,
       ∀ I : Ideal R, HasProjectiveDimensionLT (ModuleCat.of R (R ⧸ I)) n] := by
  tfae_have 1 → 4 := fun h I ↦ (iSup_projectiveDimension_lt_iff n).mp h _
  tfae_have 4 → 2 := hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_forall_ideal n
  tfae_have 2 → 3 := fun h M _ ↦ h M
  tfae_have 3 → 4 := fun h I ↦ by
    have : Small.{v} (R ⧸ I) := small_of_surjective (Submodule.mkQ_surjective I)
    exact hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_linearEquiv (Shrink.linearEquiv R (R ⧸ I)) n
      (h _ (Module.Finite.equiv (Shrink.linearEquiv R (R ⧸ I)).symm))
  tfae_have 4 → 1 := fun h ↦ (iSup_projectiveDimension_lt_iff n).mpr
    (hasProjectiveDimensionLT_of_forall_ideal n h)
  tfae_finish

end Stacks.GlobalDimension.CyclicModules

/-!
# Rings of finite global dimension: cyclic modules suffice

Let `R` be a ring and let `n : ℕ`. The global dimension of `R` (Tag 00O6) is a supremum over
*all* `R`-modules, so bounding it looks like an unbounded amount of work. This file records that
it is not: the global dimension of `R` is `< n` as soon as the cyclic modules `R ⧸ I`, one for
each ideal `I` of `R`, have projective dimension `< n`. The finite modules form an intermediate
class, giving a three-way equivalence, and the implication that carries the content is that the
cyclic modules already bound everything.

The proof is Auslander's: an arbitrary `R`-module `M` is the union of the submodules `M_e`
generated by the initial segments of a well-ordering of a set of generators of `M`, and each
graded piece `M_e / ⋃_{e' < e} M_{e'}` of that filtration is generated by one element, hence is
cyclic; so Tag 0D1U applies and bounds the projective dimension of `M` by that of the graded
pieces.

## Main results

* `ringGlobalDimension_lt_tfae`: for a ring `R` and `n : ℕ`, the global dimension of `R` is
  `< n` iff every `R`-module has projective dimension `< n` iff every finite `R`-module does iff
  every cyclic `R`-module `R ⧸ I` does.

## Implementation notes

The four items are indexed by `HasProjectiveDimensionLT _ n` rather than by the source's `≤ n`, as
in Tags 065R and 0D1U and as everywhere in `CategoryTheory.Abelian.Projective.Dimension`, since
`HasProjectiveDimensionLE M n` is by definition `HasProjectiveDimensionLT M (n + 1)`: the source's
statement is the case `n + 1`, where item (1) reads `⨆ M, projectiveDimension M ≤ n`. The extra
case `n = 0` is the true statement that `R` is the zero ring as soon as `R ⧸ ⊥` is, all its
modules being zero then. The source's *predicate* "has finite global dimension" is
`⨆ M, projectiveDimension M ≠ ⊤` (Tag 00O6), which this lemma turns into
`∃ n, ∀ I : Ideal R, HasProjectiveDimensionLE (ModuleCat.of R (R ⧸ I)) n`.

Item (1) is the source's item (1), phrased with the invariant of Tag 00O6 — the supremum
`⨆ M : ModuleCat.{u} R, projectiveDimension M`, of which `ringGlobalDimension R` is a
reducible abbreviation — rather than as a uniform bound; the two agree, since
`⨆ M, projectiveDimension M < n` holds iff `projectiveDimension M < n` for every `M` (a supremum
of values `< n` with `n` a natural number is again `< n`), which is item (2) at `v = u`. Keeping
the invariant is what makes the statement about global dimension rather than about a family of
vanishing conditions.

The modules of items (2) and (3) are taken in an arbitrary universe `v` with `Small.{v} R`, while
the supremum of item (1) and the cyclic modules `R ⧸ I` live in `R`'s own universe `u`. The source
is universe-free and is the case `v = u`, where `Small.{u} R` is automatic; the general case says
in addition that the global dimension does not depend on the universe the modules are taken in, and
costs nothing, being carried by `ModuleCat.projectiveDimension_eq_of_linearEquiv` along
`Shrink.linearEquiv`. `Small.{v} R` is the hypothesis under which `ModuleCat.{v} R` has enough
projectives — free modules on sets in `Type v`, which are the resolutions the proof builds — and is
the convention of Mathlib's projective dimension API for `ModuleCat` and of Tag 0D1U.

The source's "finite `R`-module" is `Module.Finite R M`, finitely generated; and its cyclic modules
are pinned as the quotients `R ⧸ I` of `R` by its own ideals, `I : Ideal R` ranging over all of
them, which is the same class as the modules generated by one element. Since `ModuleCat R` is the
category of *left* `R`-modules and `Ideal R` is `Submodule R R`, the statement is about left global
dimension and left ideals; the source's rings are commutative, but neither the statement nor
Auslander's argument uses commutativity, so only `[Ring R]` is assumed, as in Tags 00O6 and 0D1U.

Faithfulness to the source. (1) No hypothesis is idle: the data are `Ring R`, the source's ring,
and the bound `n`, both of which occur in every item, and `Small.{v} R`, which is what makes
`ModuleCat.{v} R` a category of `R`-modules with enough projectives and is automatic in the
source's own case `v = u`; no Noetherian, commutativity or finiteness hypothesis is added.
(2) The scalars and the base are the source's: all four items speak of modules over that same `R`
and of projective dimension in `ModuleCat R`, with no localization, quotient or completion of `R`
substituted, and the cyclic modules are quotients of `R` by its own ideals. (3) The source names no
canonical map; what it does name is pinned — the global dimension as the invariant of Tag 00O6,
the least uniform bound, rather than as the mere existence of a bound, and the cyclic modules as
`R ⧸ I` for an actual `I : Ideal R`. (4) The instantiation is not degenerate, and the equivalence
separates values of `n`: for a field `k` all four items hold at `n = 1`, every `k`-module being
free; for `R = ℤ` they all hold at `n = 2`, submodules of free `ℤ`-modules being free, and all fail
at `n = 1`, since `ℤ ⧸ (2)` is not projective; and for `R = k[x] ⧸ (x ^ 2)` they fail for every
`n`, the residue field `k = R ⧸ (x)` having infinite projective dimension, so item (3) is not a
vacuous condition on `R`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 065T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/065T)
* M. Auslander, *On the dimension of modules and algebras. III. Global dimension*,
  Nagoya Math. J. **9** (1955), 67-77 — where the argument behind Tag 0D1U is.
-/

@[expose] public section


open CategoryTheory in
/-- **Stacks 065T.** Let `R` be a ring and `n : ℕ`. The following are equivalent: the global
dimension of `R` is `< n`; every `R`-module has projective dimension `< n`; every finite
`R`-module has projective dimension `< n`; every cyclic `R`-module `R ⧸ I` has projective
dimension `< n`. The source's bound `≤ n` is the case `n + 1`. -/
@[stacks 065T "Indexed by `HasProjectiveDimensionLT _ n`, so that the source's `≤ n` is the case
`n + 1`; item (1) is the global dimension `⨆ M, projectiveDimension M` of Tag 00O6, and the
modules of items (2) and (3) range over an arbitrary universe `v` with `Small.{v} R`, of which the
source is the case `v = u`. See the module docstring.", ega "0 17.2.8" "a) <=> a')"]
public theorem ringGlobalDimension_lt_tfae (R : Type u) [Ring R] [Small.{v} R] (n : ℕ) :
    List.TFAE
      [(⨆ M : ModuleCat.{u} R, projectiveDimension M) < n,
       ∀ M : ModuleCat.{v} R, HasProjectiveDimensionLT M n,
       ∀ M : ModuleCat.{v} R, Module.Finite R M → HasProjectiveDimensionLT M n,
       ∀ I : Ideal R, HasProjectiveDimensionLT (ModuleCat.of R (R ⧸ I)) n] :=
    by apply Stacks.GlobalDimension.CyclicModules.solution

end
